<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benin City</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ubini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> City, historically known as Ubini, is a city in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and was the capital of the Benin Empire. It was the seat of the oba, his palace and the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>, and was protected by extensive earthen walls and moats. The city was captured and burned by British forces in the punitive expedition of 1897.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-city.jpg" alt="Benin City" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DipoTayo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nat_Museum,_Benin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the forest lowlands of what is now southern Nigeria, rooted in the ancient Edo settlement of Igodomigodo — later <b>Benin City</b> — which tradition holds was ruled by the Ogiso (“kings of the sky”) dynasty for several centuries before the establishment of the Oba dynasty around 1180 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Benin ranked its people around a sacred king, the Oba, who lived secluded in his palace at <b>Benin City</b> and in principle owned the kingdom’s brass, ivory and coral.</blockquote><a href="/societies/benin">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Benin</a></li><li><blockquote>Esan tradition links the founding of most chiefdoms to the 15th century, when people left <b>Benin City</b> during the reign of Oba Ewuare and settled in the forest to the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/state/esan-chiefdoms">Esan chiefdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>Craft guilds in wood and ivory and the first of the earthen walls around <b>Benin City</b> are attributed to this era.</blockquote><a href="/state/igodomigodo-ogiso-dynasty">Igodomigodo (Ogiso dynasty)</a></li><li><blockquote>Agbor lies in the forest between <b>Benin City</b> and the Niger, among the Ika, an Igboid-speaking people with strong ties to the Edo.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-agbor">Kingdom of Agbor</a></li><li><blockquote>After the British expedition of 1897 sacked <b>Benin City</b> and ended the Benin Empire, Oba Ovonramwen was exiled to Calabar.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-benin-restored">Kingdom of Benin (restored)</a></li></ul></section>
